**New evidence**
- World Bank CPF, basic services, human development, stabilization.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The CPF for 2022-2026 places strong emphasis on human development and improved access and quality of basic services, alongside governance and stabilization.","locator":"lines 146-155","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 World Bank release with concrete policy priorities and target-period fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 and squarely within the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Supports Tshisekedi-era policy direction, but the program is jointly designed and not proof of full delivery in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["This is program design, not completed outcome evidence."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- World Bank CPF, social safety net for poor and conflict-affected people.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The CPF will support a countrywide social safety net targeting poor, vulnerable, and conflict-affected people, with close to $1 billion in social protection activities benefiting about 1.2 million people.","locator":"lines 149-150","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct numeric commitment and named target groups in a major multilateral release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 and relevant to ruler-period social policy.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Tshisekedi-era government, but implementation depends heavily on World Bank and provincial partners.","contrary_evidence":["Funding commitments do not prove realized household welfare gains inside 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- World Bank CPF, girls, women, and GBV.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The CPF rebalances financing toward education and social protection, including girls' learning, women's empowerment, second-chance education, sexual and reproductive health education, and GBV support.","locator":"lines 152-153","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific vulnerable-group targeting is explicit and contemporaneous.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 CPF framing, directly relevant to target-period priorities.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Tshisekedi-era policy agenda, though delivery is shared with the Bank and local implementers.","contrary_evidence":["The release still describes a fragile, conflict-affected environment, so equitable reach is uncertain."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- World Bank CPF, governance and accountability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The CPF aims to strengthen public financial management, oversight institutions, transparency, and citizen engagement to improve accountability and service delivery.","locator":"lines 154-155","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear institutional-design claim from a major multilateral source.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022, with durable-institution relevance.","ruler_attribution":"This is government-facing governance support, but the source does not prove the president personally managed it end to end.","contrary_evidence":["Institution-building claims are forward-looking and not the same as verified service improvements."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
- World Bank budget support and transport/connectivity.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Approves $750 Million to Support Critical Governance Reforms, Transport Infrastructure, and Digital Connectivity in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/28/world-bank-approves-750-million-to-support-critical-governance-reforms-transport-infrastructure-and-digital-connectivity","claim":"The World Bank approved a $250 million development policy operation and a $500 million transport/connectivity project, and said the DRC had its first IDA-financed budget support operation since 2005.","locator":"opening summary paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct approval notice with exact financing amounts and clear policy purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Supports Tshisekedi-era reform capacity, but the money and execution are shared with external partners.","contrary_evidence":["Approval is not the same as completed delivery or broad welfare impact."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- World Bank roads, fiber, and grievance mitigation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Approves $750 Million to Support Critical Governance Reforms, Transport Infrastructure, and Digital Connectivity in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/28/world-bank-approves-750-million-to-support-critical-governance-reforms-transport-infrastructure-and-digital-connectivity","claim":"The transport/connectivity project will finance 440 kilometers of climate-resilient roads in Kasai and North Kivu and lay fiber optic infrastructure along the project roads, with measures to mitigate gender-based violence and manage grievances.","locator":"project-description paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Concrete infrastructure scope and safeguards are spelled out in the release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022 and directly relevant to social-access infrastructure.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the government development agenda, but implementation is donor-financed and shared.","contrary_evidence":["Infrastructure approval does not itself show year-end completion or equitable access."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- World Bank economic update, growth gains not broadly shared.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo Economic Update: Harnessing Growth Through Digital Connectivity to Create Jobs and Reduce Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/07/democratic-republic-of-congo-economic-update","claim":"The report says poverty reduction remained elusive and growth benefits were not broadly shared, while the DRC faced a stark digital divide and major geographic and socio-economic disparities in access.","locator":"opening summary paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct development-bank assessment with clear distributional caveats and target-year fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly an outcome/context source, limiting how much welfare credit can be assigned to the ruler.","contrary_evidence":["Strong macro growth coexisted with weak household-level gains."],"lenses":["6B.8","6B.10"]}
- World Bank economic update, telecom liberalization.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of Congo Economic Update: Harnessing Growth Through Digital Connectivity to Create Jobs and Reduce Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/07/democratic-republic-of-congo-economic-update","claim":"The report says the government liberalized the telecom sector through a new Telecommunications Law with a modern regulatory environment and a universal-access goal.","locator":"policy paragraph on telecom reform","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct policy description from a major multilateral institution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within 2022 and directly tied to government policy choices.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the government, but the release does not establish the depth of implementation by year-end.","contrary_evidence":["The same report emphasizes a persistent digital divide and unequal access."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
- WHO health emergency appeal, disease burden and vaccine response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Global Health Emergency Appeal: DRC","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-global-health-emergency-appeal-drc","claim":"WHO says DRC faced multiple communicable-disease outbreaks in 2022 and that vaccine responses vaccinated 2,635,401 people against cholera, 162,518 against meningitis, and 1,180,268 children against measles.","locator":"overview lines 217-219","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO emergency publication with explicit outbreak and response numbers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows crisis-response work under the government's watch, but the operational delivery is largely shared with WHO and partners.","contrary_evidence":["The same source shows a heavy disease burden, so response did not eliminate the underlying crisis."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- WHO COVID vaccination acceleration.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Vaccinodromes, equipes mobiles: la RDC accelere sa campagne de vaccination contre la COVID-19 ciblant 11 millions de personnes entre avril et juin 2022","publisher":"WHO Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-04-14","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/fr/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/vaccinodromes-equipes-mobiles-la-rdc-accelere-sa-campagne-de-vaccination-contre-la-covid-19-ciblant","claim":"The government launched a COVID-19 acceleration plan for April-June 2022 aimed at vaccinating at least 11 million people, while official data showed only 890,363 first doses had been given by early April.","locator":"lines 65-68","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO regional report with official campaign figures and a concrete target.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects a government-backed campaign, though actual rollout was heavily partner-supported and initial coverage remained very low.","contrary_evidence":["Low baseline coverage shows how limited the reach was at the start of the campaign."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- WHO vaccine-data triangulation and provincial measurement.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"De meilleures estimations de couverture vaccinale grace au renforcement des capacites locales sur la triangulation des donnees en RDC","publisher":"WHO Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/node/16479","claim":"WHO and local health teams used provincial triangulation of vaccine data to improve routine immunization estimates, data quality, and program planning, and the work reported official 2009-2021 triangulated coverage estimates.","locator":"lines 406-419","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct explanation of the measurement process, data gaps, and institutional correction effort.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 and relevant to the year’s service-delivery management.","ruler_attribution":"This supports institutional correction under the health ministry, but it is not a personal presidential action.","contrary_evidence":["The source also highlights major data and coverage gaps, so it is not a clean success story."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
- UNICEF refugee WASH and protection response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"An integrated response for Central African refugee populations","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-02-16","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/stories/integrated-response-central-african-refugee-populations","claim":"UNICEF says it quickly put in place a water, hygiene and sanitation response coupled with child and gender-based violence protection measures, improving access to clean water for more than 47,000 people and adequate sanitation for more than 35,000.","locator":"lines 73-90 and 107-109","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 field report with concrete outputs and vulnerable-group targeting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is mostly partner-led humanitarian delivery under government watch, with indirect state attribution through coordination and access.","contrary_evidence":["The response was needed because host communities started from low access to safe water and sanitation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}
- UNICEF crisis education funding and girls focus.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Education Cannot Wait and UNICEF with partners call for substantial increase in funding for crisis-affected children in the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/press-releases/call-substantial-increase-funding-crisis-affected-children","claim":"The joint programme said about 3.2 million children aged 6 to 11 were out of school, targeted 67,000 children and adolescents in Tanganyika, had already reached 32,000 with quality education, and supported the government's free-education drive.","locator":"lines 65-76 and 88-94","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 education press release with target groups, reach numbers, and policy direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This supports the government-linked education agenda, but implementation is shared with UNICEF, ECW, WFP, and provincial authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The same release shows very large out-of-school numbers and thin coverage relative to need."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF urgent needs in Rutshuru and Nyiragongo.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La vie et le bien-etre des enfants sont menacees a Rutshuru et Nyiragongo en RDC","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-07-30","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/communiques-presse/vie-bien-etre-enfants-sont-menaces-rutshuru-nyiragongo","claim":"Since March 2022, more than 190,000 people, half of them children, were forced to flee in Rutshuru and Nyiragongo, and UNICEF said families urgently needed food, health care, water, hygiene, and sanitation.","locator":"lines 69-74","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 field report with concrete displacement and service-needs claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mainly crisis context and humanitarian response; presidential responsibility is indirect through security and response capacity.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a humanitarian alarm, so it shows need more than government success."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF mass displacement and emergency response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF: Mass displacements in eastern DRC pose a deadly threat to children and young people","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-11-07","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/unicef-mass-displacements-eastern-drc-pose-a-deadly-threat-children-and-young-people","claim":"UNICEF said about 200,000 IDPs had fled since late March 2022, warned of cholera risk from clean-water shortages, and continued WASH, health, nutrition, and education assistance for the most vulnerable.","locator":"lines 197-207 and 201-204","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 UNICEF emergency report with concrete displacement and service-response detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The crisis reflects conditions under the administration's watch, but the service response is largely UNICEF-led and partner-delivered.","contrary_evidence":["The source emphasizes that lives remained in grave danger and aid needs were still rising."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF school closures and temporary learning spaces.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The dilemma of schools that shelter DRC’s displaced families","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2023-09-02","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/stories/dilemma-schools-shelter-drcs-displaced-families","claim":"UNICEF said over 2,000 schools had closed since January 2022 because of insecurity and displacement, and that UNICEF and partners had built 428 temporary learning spaces while only 2 percent of education-funding needs were pledged.","locator":"lines 59-105","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Retrospective but specific, with direct education-disruption and response figures tied to the 2022 crisis period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on January 2022 onward, so it fits the target period strongly.","ruler_attribution":"The schooling disruption is mostly conflict-driven, but the state's capacity to protect education and reopen schools remains relevant.","contrary_evidence":["UNICEF also documents temporary learning spaces and advocacy, so the picture is not pure abandonment."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.10"]}
- WFP malnutrition and food insecurity.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"France helps address child malnutrition in the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://www.wfp.org/news/france-helps-address-child-malnutrition-democratic-republic-congo","claim":"WFP said 2.4 million children under five and 1.7 million pregnant and nursing women were suffering acute malnutrition, with 45 percent of under-five deaths related to malnutrition, while WFP and partners had treated some 1.4 million malnourished children and women by October 2022.","locator":"lines 257-265","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 WFP release with hard numbers on malnutrition and response scale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is mostly crisis and donor-response evidence, but it captures the scale of unmet need under the ruler-period.","contrary_evidence":["The same source shows the crisis remained severe and assistance was still insufficient."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across World Bank, WHO, UNICEF, WFP, ECW, and a few related DRC pages.
- I kept the strongest non-duplicative sources that added new material claims, not paraphrases of the same release.
- I rejected duplicate pages and index pages that only repeated the same program headlines without new target-year substance.
- I rejected out-of-period 2024-2026 appeal/homepage pages when they did not add a precise retrospective 2022 claim.
- I left pure conflict, casualty, and diplomacy material to the peace, safety, and international-relations chapters unless it directly affected schooling, health, WASH, nutrition, or social protection.
- I did not re-fetch local structured priors, and I did not use the client matrix.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct household-level outcome evidence is still thin. I have strong program and response evidence, but less on measured changes in mortality, learning, or nutrition attributable to Tshisekedi in 2022.
- Budget execution and implementation quality in the social sectors remain under-documented relative to announcements and donor commitments.
- There is still limited independent evidence on subnational distribution, especially east versus other regions, beyond crisis-zone reporting.
- Durable institutional change is visible in data-planning and social-safety-net design, but I do not yet have strong post-implementation audits proving permanence.
- If you want a tighter dossier, the next useful search would be audits, household surveys, and sector evaluations for health, education, and social protection rather than more press releases.
